    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Vis-NIR spectroscopy for rapid soil carbon and nitrogen estimation in humid tropical landscape","description":"Carbon is a key component of soil health, playing a crucial role in nutrient cycling, microbial activity, and soil fertility. Its distribution varies significantly across different landscapes due to variations in soil forming factors and land use practices. Conventional soil analysis methods are labour intensive, time consuming, and costly, stressing the need for rapid and cost effective alternatives. This study aimed to assess the spatial variability of soil organic carbon (SOC), total carbon (TC), total nitrogen (TN), and available nitrogen (Av. N) across diverse landscapes, and to evaluate the predictive potential of Visible-Near Infrared (Vis-NIR) reflectance spectroscopy for estimating soil carbon and nitrogen fractions. A total of 210 georeferenced surface soil samples (0–20 cm depth) were collected from three Agroecological (AEU) landscapes: Kole lands (AEU 6), North Central Laterite (AEU 10), and Palakkad Central Plain (AEU 22). Standard laboratory procedures were employed to determine SOC, TC, TN, and Av. N. and soil reflectance spectra were acquired using an ASD FieldSpec spectroradiometer. The spectral data were pre-processed using multiple techniques, including first and second-order derivatives (FOD and SOD), continuum removal (CR), and logarithmic transformation [log(1/R)]. The pre-processed spectral data, along with laboratory measured soil properties, were used to develop predictive models. Partial Least Squares Regression (PLSR), Support Vector Regression (SVR), and Random Forest (RF) models were applied to establish relationships between spectral signatures and soil properties. Model performance was evaluated using coefficient of determination (R²), root mean square error (RMSE), and residual prediction deviation (RPD). Among the models, SVR demonstrated superior predictive performance, with R² values of 0.75 for SOC, 0.75 for TC, 0.68 for TN, and 0.55 for Av. N. These results highlight the strong potential of Vis-NIR spectroscopy, particularly when combined with SVR, as a rapid and reliable alternative to conventional laboratory methods. This approach can significantly support large scale soil monitoring and precision agriculture applications across similar agro-ecological landscapes.","datePublished":"2026-09-12T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-09-12T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"1","pageEnd":"18","sameAs":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10333-026-01095-2","keywords":["Vis-NIR spectroscopy","Agro Ecological Unit","Soil organic carbon","Total carbon","Available nitrogen","Total nitrogen","Agriculture","Hydrology/Water Resources","Hydrogeology","Geoecology/Natural Processes","Ecotoxicology","Soil Science & Conservation"],"image":["https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10333-026-01095-2/MediaObjects/10333_2026_1095_Fig1_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10333-026-01095-2/MediaObjects/10333_2026_1095_Fig2_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10333-026-01095-2/MediaObjects/10333_2026_1095_Fig3_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10333-026-01095-2/MediaObjects/10333_2026_1095_Fig4_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10333-026-01095-2/MediaObjects/10333_2026_1095_Fig5_HTML.png"],"isPartOf":{"name":"Paddy and Water Environment","issn":["1611-2504","1611-2490"],"@type":["Periodical"]},"publisher":{"name":"Springer Nature Singapore","logo":{"url":"https://www.springernature.com/app-sn/public/images/logo-springernature.png","@type":"ImageObject"},"@type":"Organization"},"author":[{"name":"Nandaluru Kalpana","affiliation":[{"name":"Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Department of Soil Science & Agricultural Chemistry, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Divya Vijayan Vattekat","affiliation":[{"name":"Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Department of Remote sensing & GIS, College of Forestry, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"email":"divya.vijayan@kau.in","@type":"Person"},{"name":"Tarik Mitran","affiliation":[{"name":"National Remote Sensing Centre","address":{"name":"Soil & Land Resources Assessment Division, National Remote Sensing Centre, Hyderabad, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Beena Viswanathan Indira","affiliation":[{"name":"Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Department of Soil Science & Agricultural Chemistry, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Geetha Pattath","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Agriculture, Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Soil Science & Agrl. Chemistry, Radiotracer Laboratory, College of Agriculture, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Sajitha Vijayan Mattery","affiliation":[{"name":"Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Department of Agricultural Statistics, College of Agriculture, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Vishnu Baburaj Reeja","affiliation":[{"name":"Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Agrl. Statistics, College of Forestry, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Abdul Hadi Kaipakasseri","affiliation":[{"name":"Kerala Agricultural University","address":{"name":"Department of Soil Science & Agricultural Chemistry, Kerala Agricultural University, Thrissur, India","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"}],"isAccessibleForFree":false,"hasPart":{"isAccessibleForFree":false,"cssSelector":".main-content","@type":"WebPageElement"},"@type":"ScholarlyArticle"},"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage"}</script>

        
        
